RQ1 effect. Again for completeness, repeating the old plots with 2 different fixed values of sigma. With lower sigma, L1 infers lower means from the long utterance vs the null. For higher sigma they are about the same, reproducing the hyperprior over mu (which makes sense because if the listener thinks the speaker thinks the standard deviation is very high, her utterance choice will not really help the him infer mu, since she'll generally prefer the reasons in any case).

```{r}

#see how different combinations of the hyperpriors affect the inference
hyperparams <- expand.grid(mean_mu = mu_vals, mean_sigma = sigma_vals)

#lot of copied code i'll edit it down later
# also this takes a while to run sorry
L1_probs <- mapply(function (mean_mu, mean_sigma) {
  
  hyperprior_mu <- dnorm(mu_vals, mean_mu, 5)
  p_mu <- hyperprior_mu[mu_sigma_grid$mu_j]
  p_mu <- p_mu / (sum(p_mu) * dMu)
  
  hyperprior_sigma <- dnorm(sigma_vals, mean_sigma, 5)
  p_sigma <- hyperprior_sigma[mu_sigma_grid$sigma_k]
  p_sigma <- p_sigma / (sum(p_sigma) * dSigma)
  
  #L1(mu, sigma|R) = speaker choice probs * hyperpriors
  joint_null_unnorm <- mu_sigma_grid$prob_null * p_mu * p_sigma 
  joint_long_unnorm <- mu_sigma_grid$prob_long * p_mu * p_sigma 
  
  mu_sigma_grid$joint_null <- joint_null_unnorm / (sum(joint_null_unnorm) * dMu * dSigma)
  mu_sigma_grid$joint_long <- joint_long_unnorm / (sum(joint_long_unnorm) * dMu * dSigma)
  
  
  L0_matrix_null <- matrix(L0_array_null, nrow = length(V_vals)) 
  L0_matrix_long <- matrix(L0_array_long, nrow = length(V_vals))
  
  #conditionalization from new prior
  p_V_null <- (L0_matrix_null %*% mu_sigma_grid$joint_null) * dMu * dSigma
  p_V_long <- (L0_matrix_long %*% mu_sigma_grid$joint_long) * dMu * dSigma


  #expected value of each distribution
  c(EV_null = sum(V_vals * p_V_null),
    EV_long = sum(V_vals * p_V_long))
  
}, hyperparams$mean_mu, hyperparams$mean_sigma)

EV_df <- data.frame(
  mu = hyperparams$mean_mu,
  nu = hyperparams$mean_sigma,
  EV_null = L1_probs["EV_null", ],
  EV_long = L1_probs["EV_long", ]
)

```

```{r}
ev_plot <- EV_df %>%
  mutate(EV_diff = log(EV_long - EV_null)) #to make the difference show up a little more, the backfire is quite small with this particular choice of parameters

ggplot(ev_plot, aes(x = mu, y = nu, fill = EV_diff)) +
    geom_tile() +
    scale_fill_gradient2(low = "red", mid = "white", high = "blue", midpoint = 0) +
    labs(title = "L1 expected value diff",  x = "mu", y = "sigma", fill = "") +
    theme_minimal()
```

RQ2 effect. Also seems sensible that the backfire should only occur when the hyperprior has high mean/low standard deviation (i.e. where the listener would strongly agree even without the reasons). However, the effect is very small (needed a log scale to make it show up well in the heatmap) and occurs only for very high means. Perhaps fussing with cost, alpha, and especially the likelihood can create a stronger effect?
